In article <1991Feb26.234444.20482@chinet.chi.il.us> prb@chinet.chi.il.us (Paul Botts) writes:

>Well, so he's right - there is no (or virtually no) effective difference
>between strength of 18/01 and 18/50 and 25. Why not? If this is the
>case, why bother with the higher strength numbers at all? And why
>shouldn't people who have died after wishing for gloves of power
>(instead of something actually useful) be extraordinarily ticked off?

Well, this isn't quite true.  There's virtually no difference between
18/** and 25.  There's a fair amount of difference between 18/01-18/50
and 25 as far as hit/damage bonuses go.  As the following table will
illustrate:


        Strength        To Hit      Damage
          3-5             -2          -1
          6-7             -1           0
          8-15             0           0
          16               0          +1
          17              +1          +1
          18              +1          +2
          18/01-18/49     +1          +3
          18/50-18/74     +2          +3
          18/75-18/89     +2          +4
          18/90-18/99     +2          +5
          18/** (25)      +3          +6

So, 25 is +2 better to hit than 18/01 (and +1 better to hit than 18/50),
and +3 damage better than both 18/01 and 18/50.  

As far as gauntlets of power being useful, they're much more useful for
classes which start out with strength around 9.  Strength can be enhanced,
but it *does* take a lot of giant corpses and the like.  And the lowered
carrying capacity of, say, a wizard can be a pain to deal with (not to
mention the vastly lessened hit/damage bonuses).  For those classes,
gauntlets of power may be just the thing.  Of course, gauntlets of dexterity
might be even better....

